WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.545
Here’s how to run the personal user manual.

00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:07.854
It takes about twenty-five minutes.

00:00:07.904 --> 00:00:12.058
Show the six prompts: You get the best from me when… I struggle when…

00:00:12.108 --> 00:00:13.725
I prefer feedback that…

00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:15.822
Under pressure, I may…

00:00:15.873 --> 00:00:17.092
Some of my quirks are…

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:19.917
And: You can rely on me for…

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:24.822
Start by sharing your own manual. This is important.

00:00:24.872 --> 00:00:34.486
As the leader, going first with an honest answer, especially to “Under pressure, I may…”, sets the level of openness for everyone else.

00:00:34.536 --> 00:00:37.219
If you share something real, they will too.

00:00:37.269 --> 00:00:40.762
Then give everyone ten minutes to write their own, alone.

00:00:40.812 --> 00:00:45.613
One post-it per prompt, with their initials, stuck on the canvas in the right box.

00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:47.745
Quiet writing time matters here.

00:00:47.795 --> 00:00:51.306
These are personal questions, and people need a moment to think.

00:00:51.355 --> 00:00:53.514
Next, put people in pairs.

00:00:53.564 --> 00:00:55.828
Each person talks through their manual.

00:00:55.878 --> 00:00:59.901
The listener asks one question to understand better, not to challenge.

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:04.138
For example: “What does good feedback look like for you, concretely?”

00:01:04.188 --> 00:01:09.495
Finally, come back together and ask each pair for one surprising or useful insight.

00:01:09.545 --> 00:01:11.826
Not the whole manual, just one thing.

00:01:11.876 --> 00:01:16.210
You’ll often hear things like: “I had no idea you preferred written feedback.”

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.683
Those small discoveries are the point.

00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:25.196
After the session, keep the manuals somewhere everyone can find them, and tell new joiners to write theirs in their first week.

00:01:25.246 --> 00:01:30.368
A manual is most useful three months later, when someone is about to give feedback and remembers to check.

